Re-weight the Lacis and Hansen shortwave water vapour absorptance for a
non-solar host, adding `h2osww` to `radmod_nl` beside `o3uvw` and `o3visw`.

WHAT IT CHANGES. Lacis and Hansen (1974) Eq. 21 is a fit to Yamamoto (1962), and
Yamamoto defines his absorptivity as "the ratio to the solar constant of the
energy absorbed by the entire vertical air column" -- a fraction of TOTAL
incident flux, built by weighting laboratory band absorptivities with the SOLAR
flux. `swr` divides it by `zsolar2` and multiplies it back by band-2 flux, so the
two cancel and the absorbed flux is the solar value whatever the star. A redder
host puts more of its flux where water vapour absorbs and gets the Sun's
absorption anyway. This patch multiplies the absorptance by `h2osww` at all three
sites: the initial upward-beam total, the downward beam per layer, and the upward
scattered beam per layer. `h2osww = 1.0` is the default and reproduces Lacis and
Hansen exactly, so a solar-host run is bit-identical.

Derivation of the weight, and the checks that could have failed:
`exoplasim/scripts/shortwave_band_weights.py` and
`exoplasim/notes/shortwave-water-vapour.md`.

THE BASE IT APPLIES TO, and it is not pristine 3.4.2. Like the star-cycle patch,
this one goes on top of the ozone band weights, so its base is
"3.4.2 + exoplasim-3.4.2-ozone-band-weights.patch":

    pristine 3.4.2                              eb8e9e1c0127940e607828899ff5dea6653c9835c...
    + exoplasim-3.4.2-ozone-band-weights.patch  7fd39458a87a0bc042d17b0b93c2e45cbfc4fc04a560965c974efa734019c0ba
    + this patch                                40bc4571b710ccd17d0ce420ceb9b3e5f177757e8548a249389f52f2a4a2062e

Verify before applying, and refuse if it does not match:

    sha256sum .venv/lib/python3.12/site-packages/exoplasim/plasim/src/radmod.f90
    patch --forward --strip=1 --directory=.venv/lib/python3.12/site-packages/exoplasim < exoplasim/patches/exoplasim-3.4.2-h2o-shortwave-weight.patch

WHAT MUST LAND WITH IT, in this order. This patch alone is inert and applying it
alone breaks things, so none of these is optional:

  1. add it to `RESIDENT_PATCHES` in `exoplasim/scripts/rebuild_binaries.py`
  2. apply it, then `python exoplasim/scripts/rebuild_binaries.py`, then
     `--verify`. It adds a NAMELIST KEY, so every unrebuilt binary aborts in
     `radini_` with "Cannot match namelist object name h2osww" --
     `notes/failure-modes.md` class 11, which this failure mode is the archetype
     of and which is why it is survivable
  3. `build_star_cycle_exoplasim.sh` pins `base_sha` at the line above and will
     refuse to run. The star-cycle patch must be REGENERATED against the new
     base, not merely re-pinned; its own comment says so
  4. add `model.h2o_sw_weight` to `config/planet.yaml` and set `H2OSWW` from it
     in `run_exoplasim.py` and `continue_exoplasim.py`, the way `O3UVW` and
     `O3VISW` are already set. Setting the namelist key BEFORE the rebuild is
     what breaks every run, so this step follows the rebuild
  5. CHOOSE THE VALUE TO MATCH THE SPECTRUM THE RUN USES. No run on this build
     has been reading k25v -- `NSTARFILE` is 0 and `solarini` builds a 4965 K
     Planck curve instead, which is PHYS-2 -- and the weight is 1.202 for that
     blackbody against 1.346 for the real spectrum. Setting 1.346 while the model
     runs a blackbody over-corrects by 12%
  6. re-run the baseline, and re-derive the flux: the correction is predicted to
     move the mean off its design value by 1.45 K on the blackbody or 2.5 K with
     the spectrum declared. If this patch, the spectrum and SPEC-2 all land in
     one rebuild, run two short attribution segments with `h2osww` off and on
     before the long one, or nothing in the result can be attributed to anything

--- a/plasim/src/radmod.f90
+++ b/plasim/src/radmod.f90
@@ -57,6 +57,22 @@
 !     bit-identical.
       real    :: o3uvw   = 1.0    ! weight, Hartley-Huggins UV terms
       real    :: o3visw  = 1.0    ! weight, Chappuis visible term
+!     The same correction in the larger term. Lacis & Hansen's water vapour
+!     absorptance is their Eq. 21, a fit to Yamamoto (1962), and Yamamoto states
+!     the definition outright: the ratio to the SOLAR CONSTANT of the energy
+!     absorbed by the whole air column. It is a fraction of total incident flux
+!     and the Sun's spectrum is inside it, because Yamamoto built it by weighting
+!     laboratory band absorptivities with the solar flux and summing them.
+!
+!     Dividing by zsolar2 below converts it to a fraction of band-2 flux and then
+!     multiplies it back by band-2 flux, so the two cancel and the ABSORBED FLUX
+!     is Lacis & Hansen's solar value for any star at all. A redder host puts
+!     more of its flux in the near infrared where water vapour absorbs, and gets
+!     the Sun's absorption anyway. h2osww is that band re-weighting.
+!
+!     Default 1.0 reproduces Lacis & Hansen exactly, so a solar-host run is
+!     bit-identical.
+      real    :: h2osww  = 1.0    ! weight, near-infrared H2O bands
       integer :: no3     = 1      ! switch for ozon (0=no,1=yes,2=datafile)
       integer :: nsol    = 1      ! switch for solang (1/0=yes/no)
       integer :: nswr    = 1      ! switch for swr (1/0=yes/no)
@@ -614,7 +630,7 @@
 !
